Let $t$ be time in hours and $k>0$. The evaporation model is $\dfrac{dV}{dt}=-k\pi r^2$.
Similar triangles give $\dfrac hr=\dfrac{18}{6}=3$, so $h=3r$ and $V=\pi r^3$.
The diagram shows an axial cross-section; the inner horizontal segment is the water surface.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2.5mm,y=2.5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (-6,18)--(0,0)--(6,18)--cycle;
\draw[black] (-4,12)--(4,12);
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(0,18);
\draw[black,<->] (7,0)--(7,18);
\path (7,9) node[right] {$18\ \mathrm{cm}$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,19)--(6,19);
\path (3,19) node[above] {$6\ \mathrm{cm}$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,13)--(4,13);
\path (2,13) node[above] {$r$};
\draw[black,<->] (-5,0)--(-5,12);
\path (-5,6) node[left] {$h$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
By the chain rule:
$\begin{align*}\dfrac{dV}{dt}&=\dfrac{dV}{dr}\dfrac{dr}{dt}\\ 3\pi r^2\dfrac{dr}{dt}&=-k\pi r^2\\ \dfrac{dr}{dt}&=-\dfrac k3\end{align*}$
The cancellation applies while $r>0$. Integrating and using $r(0)=6$ gives $r=6-\dfrac k3t$.
At $t=4$, $h=15$, so $r=5$.
$\begin{align*}5&=6-\dfrac{4k}{3}\\ k&=\dfrac34\\ r&=6-\dfrac t4\end{align*}$
The tank empties when $r=0$, after $24$ hours. The radius formula applies for $0\le t\le24$.
The model assumes constant evaporation per unit surface area; temperature and humidity may change.
